<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Fit Pixels, Get Labels: Meta-Learned Implicit Networks for Image Segmentation</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Kushal Vyas
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ashok Veeraraghavan
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Guha Balakrishnan
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author"><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Rice University, Houston
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">E-mail </span><a href="mailto:%7Bkvyas,vashok,guha%7D@rice.edu">{kvyas,vashok,guha}@rice.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Implicit neural representations (INRs) have achieved remarkable successes in learning expressive yet compact signal representations. However, they are not naturally amenable to predictive tasks such as segmentation, where they must learn semantic structures over a distribution of signals. In this study, we introduce MetaSeg, a meta-learning framework to train INRs for medical image segmentation. MetaSeg uses an underlying INR that simultaneously predicts per pixel intensity values and class labels. It then uses a meta-learning procedure to find optimal initial parameters for this INR over a training dataset of images and segmentation maps, such that the INR can simply be fine-tuned to fit pixels of an unseen test image, and automatically decode its class labels. We evaluated MetaSeg on 2D and 3D brain MRI segmentation tasks and report Dice scores comparable to commonly used U-Net models, but with <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90\%</annotation></semantics></math> fewer parameters. MetaSeg offers a fresh, scalable alternative to traditional resource-heavy architectures such as U-Nets and vision transformers for medical image segmentation. Our project is available <a href="https://kushalvyas.github.io/metaseg.html" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">here</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Medical Image Segmentation, Implicit Neural Representations, Meta-Learning, MRI
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Implicit neural representations (INRs) have demonstrated impressive performance in encoding complex natural signals. A typical INR <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a multilayer perceptron (MLP) with parameters <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> that maps a coordinate
<math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in\mathrm{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in\mathrm{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> to signal value <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\mathbf{x})\in\mathrm{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\mathbf{x})\in\mathrm{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and is
iteratively fit
to a specific signal.
Because they offer continuous signal representations, excellent reconstruction performance, and implicit signal priors, INRs have gained significant attention in the computer vision community for compactly modeling large signals<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, and in the medical imaging community for inverse imaging tasks such as accelerated MRI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, and sparse-view CT reconstruction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. Despite these successes, learned INR representations are highly specific to a given signal and to the way its parameters are initialized. As a result, unlike architectures such as U-Nets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> and vision transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, INR-produced features lack structural or semantic coherence, making them less used for tasks requiring learning over an image distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Results from recent studies suggest that this shortcoming of INRs may be fixable. In particular, by fitting an INR across multiple images starting from the same parameter intialization, the final parameters exhibit clear semantic, structural properties for datasets such as faces or MRI scans <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. And as a result, these learned initializations allow the INR to fit unseen (test) images rapidly with far fewer gradient updates while learning more generalizable features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. In this study, we leverage this insight to develop an INR fitting strategy for medical image segmentation. In particular, we present the surprising discovery that an INR carefully optimized to fit many training pairs of images and associated segmentation maps can predict a segmentation map for an unseen test image <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">when simply fine-tuned to reconstruct the image’s pixels.</em></p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04021v1/cover_figure_v10.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/195;" width="476" height="195" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of MetaSeg.</span> (a) We use a meta-learning framework to learn optimal initial parameters <math id="S1.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*},\phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*},\phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for an INR consisting of an <math id="S1.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer reconstruction network <math id="S1.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and shallow segmentation head <math id="S1.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>. (b) At test time, optimally initialized INR <math id="S1.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> is iteratively fit to the pixels of an unseen test scan. After convergence, the penultimate features <math id="S1.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta^{*}}^{L-1}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{*}}^{L-1}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> are fed as input to the segmentation head <math id="S1.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}^{*}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}^{*}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to predict per-pixel class labels.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We propose MetaSeg, a novel meta-learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> INR framework that performs this strategy. MetaSeg uses an INR that simultaneously predicts both image pixels and segmentation labels given a coordinate (Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Fit Pixels, Get Labels: Meta-Learned Implicit Networks for Image Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>b), and aims to find an optimal setting of the INR parameters (<math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>), such that the network can be quickly fine-tuned to fit any image-mask pair starting from these parameters. MetaSeg uses a nested, meta-learning procedure (Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Fit Pixels, Get Labels: Meta-Learned Implicit Networks for Image Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>a). In the inner optimization, the INR is fit to reconstruct a randomly selected batch of training image and segmentation mask pairs. The outer (meta) optimization applies a gradient descent step to the current values of <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> based on the converged inner parameters. These steps iterate to converge to an initialization that works well for both image reconstruction and segmentation objectives from the given image distribution. At test time, we simply fit the INR initialized with the learned optimal parameters to the test image (without needing any segmentation information), generating viable segmentation masks in just <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> gradient descent updates and near state-of-the-art quality masks after <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> updates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">MetaSeg draws inspiration from Neural Implicit Segmentation Functions (NISFs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, which also use an INR to predict pixel and segmentation values. However, instead of forcing the INR to learn image-label correlations, NISF takes an additional latent vector as input to represent an image’s semantic content, which must then be optimized at test time to produce a mask. Due to this strategy, NISF must apply several regularization losses on INR parameters and the latent space to succeed. In contrast, MetaSeg offers a much simpler training methodology based on meta-learning, only requiring reconstruction and segmentation losses during training and only a reconstruction loss during testing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated MetaSeg on 2D and 3D magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) segmentation tasks using the OASIS-MRI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> dataset. We first find that MetaSeg achieves similar performance for 2D (Dice score: <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.93" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.93</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.93</annotation></semantics></math>) and 3D (Dice score: <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.91" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.91</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.91</annotation></semantics></math>) MRI segmentation tasks to widely used U-Net baselines <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> with <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90\%</annotation></semantics></math> fewer parameters, and significantly outperforms NISF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. Second, we show that MetaSeg performs well for fine-grained segmentation tasks,
accurately segmenting image regions which may be sparse and span only a few pixels.
Third, we show that when trained on low-resolution scans, MetaSeg fares better on high-resolution scans than U-Nets, likely due to its continuous representation. Finally, we qualitatively demonstrate through principal component analysis (PCA) visualizations that MetaSeg’s learned initial parameters encode semantic information correlated with ground truth segmentations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Methods</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assume a given dataset <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{I_{j},S_{j}\}_{j=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{I_{j},S_{j}\}_{j=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> subjects, where <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are a
<math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional image scan and segmentation map, respectively (<math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> in our experiments). At location <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{j}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{j}(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{j}(x)\in\{0,1\}^{|\mathcal{C}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{j}(x)\in\{0,1\}^{|\mathcal{C}|}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of image channels, and <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We propose the MetaSeg framework to estimate <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{S}</annotation></semantics></math> for an unseen scan <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> at test time. MetaSeg consists of an INR (Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Fit Pixels, Get Labels: Meta-Learned Implicit Networks for Image Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>b) with an <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-layer MLP
<math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}:\mathrm{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathrm{R}^{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>D</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}:\mathrm{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathrm{R}^{D}</annotation></semantics></math> that predicts <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{I}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{I}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>
given coordinate <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a segmentation head <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}:\mathrm{R}^{h}\rightarrow\mathrm{R}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}:\mathrm{R}^{h}\rightarrow\mathrm{R}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math> that predicts <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> class probabilities given the penultimate features, of dimensionality <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, computed by <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{L-1}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{L-1}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math>. At test time, we aim to fit <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> steps, and then simply compute <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{S}(\mathbf{x})=g_{\phi}(f^{L-1}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{S}(\mathbf{x})=g_{\phi}(f^{L-1}_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}))</annotation></semantics></math> in one feed-forward operation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Sec. <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Training: Meta-Learning and Segmentation Head Optimization ‣ 2 Methods ‣ Fit Pixels, Get Labels: Meta-Learned Implicit Networks for Image Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>, we describe MetaSeg’s strategy for learning optimal values <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*},\phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*},\phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta^{*}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{*}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi^{*}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi^{*}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> (networks initialized with <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>) may be easily fine-tuned to reconstruct and segment a scan from the distribution. In Sec. <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Inference: Fit Pixels, Get Labels ‣ 2 Methods ‣ Fit Pixels, Get Labels: Meta-Learned Implicit Networks for Image Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, we describe how the INR initialized with these parameters may be used at inference time to estimate a segmentation map for a scan.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Training: Meta-Learning and Segmentation Head Optimization</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">MetaSeg uses a MAML<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> meta-learning strategy to learn optimal parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*},\phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*},\phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, consisting of a nested optimization with <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inner</em> and <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">outer</em> (meta) routines (Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Fit Pixels, Get Labels: Meta-Learned Implicit Networks for Image Segmentation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>a). Starting with initial random values <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> at iteration <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the outer routine takes <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> gradient descent steps to move the parameters to generalizable values. The gradient at each outer step is based on the results of the inner routine, which fits <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta^{t}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{t}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi^{t}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi^{t}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> steps on one data pair <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(I_{j},S_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(I_{j},S_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>. The outer routine uses the final inner parameter values as a gradient signal to update <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{t},\phi^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{t},\phi^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. To avoid a noisy gradient due to overfitting to example <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, we set <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to a small value in practice (e.g., <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}=2</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the inner routine, at time step <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we optimize both <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta^{t}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{t}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi^{t}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi^{t}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> for a single subject <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> with a loss function:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{inner}(I_{j},\hat{I}_{j},S_{j},\hat{S}_{j})=\sum_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{L}_{recon}(I_{j}(\mathbf{x}),\hat{I}_{j}(\mathbf{x}))+\mathcal{L}_{cls}(S_{j}(\mathbf{x}),\hat{S}_{j}(\mathbf{x}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{inner}(I_{j},\hat{I}_{j},S_{j},\hat{S}_{j})=\sum_{\mathbf{x}}\mathcal{L}_{recon}(I_{j}(\mathbf{x}),\hat{I}_{j}(\mathbf{x}))+\mathcal{L}_{cls}(S_{j}(\mathbf{x}),\hat{S}_{j}(\mathbf{x})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{recon}(\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b})=\mid\mid\mathbf{a}-\mathbf{b}\mid\mid^{2}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∣</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐛</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∣</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.0835em">∣</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{recon}(\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b})=\mid\mid\mathbf{a}-\mathbf{b}\mid\mid^{2}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a per-pixel reconstruction loss, and</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{cls}(\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b})=\sum_{c=1}^{C}-(1-\mathbf{b}(c)){{}^{\gamma}}\cdot\delta_{c,\mathbf{a}}\log(\mathbf{b}(c))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></munderover><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mmultiscripts><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mi>γ</mi></mmultiscripts><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{cls}(\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b})=\sum_{c=1}^{C}-(1-\mathbf{b}(c)){{}^{\gamma}}\cdot\delta_{c,\mathbf{a}}\log(\mathbf{b}(c))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">is a per-pixel multiclass focal classification loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter. We use a focal loss to account for heavy class imbalance across pixels. In the outer step, we make a gradient descent update:</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\theta^{t+1},\phi^{t+1}]\leftarrow[\theta^{t},\phi^{t}]-\beta\nabla_{[\theta^{t},\phi^{t}]}\big(\mathcal{L}_{inner}(I_{j},\hat{I}_{j},S_{j},\hat{S}_{j})\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>I</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\theta^{t+1},\phi^{t+1}]\leftarrow[\theta^{t},\phi^{t}]-\beta\nabla_{[\theta^{t},\phi^{t}]}\big(\mathcal{L}_{inner}(I_{j},\hat{I}_{j},S_{j},\hat{S}_{j})\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where gradient <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{[\theta^{t},\phi^{t}]}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{[\theta^{t},\phi^{t}]}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> computes the difference between the converged parameters (<math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{t}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{t}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{t}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{t}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>) of the inner optimization and current parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{t},\phi^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{t},\phi^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. After <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> outer gradient descent steps, we obtain parameters <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{T_{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{T_{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{T_{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{T_{o}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We freeze <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{*}=\theta^{T_{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{*}=\theta^{T_{o}}</annotation></semantics></math>, but further optimize <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{T_{o}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{T_{o}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that when, at test time, <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta^{*}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{*}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is fit for <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> iterations on any random scan, we can obtain accurate segmentation predictions, in one step.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To do so, we first fit <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta^{*}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{*}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> separately on each training scan <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> iterations and populate a dataset of learned scan-specific features and segmentation maps, <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{f_{\theta^{*}_{j}}^{L-1}(\mathbf{x}),S_{j}(\mathbf{x})\}_{j=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{f_{\theta^{*}_{j}}^{L-1}(\mathbf{x}),S_{j}(\mathbf{x})\}_{j=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then globally optimize <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> by minimizing the loss function <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{seg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{seg}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{seg}(\mathcal{D})=\sum_{\mathbf{x}}\sum_{j}\mathcal{L}_{cls}(S_{j}(\mathbf{x}),g_{\phi}(f_{\theta^{*}_{j}}^{L-1}(\mathbf{x})))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{seg}(\mathcal{D})=\sum_{\mathbf{x}}\sum_{j}\mathcal{L}_{cls}(S_{j}(\mathbf{x}),g_{\phi}(f_{\theta^{*}_{j}}^{L-1}(\mathbf{x}))).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">We freeze the converged parameters as <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Inference: Fit Pixels, Get Labels</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At inference time, we assume a given (unseen) scan <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>. We fit <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta^{*}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{*}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, solely optimizing the pixelwise reconstruction loss <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{recon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{recon}</annotation></semantics></math>. Upon completion, we compute <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{S}(\mathbf{x})=g_{\phi^{*}}(f^{L-1}_{\theta^{*}}(\mathbf{x}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{S}(\mathbf{x})=g_{\phi^{*}}(f^{L-1}_{\theta^{*}}(\mathbf{x}))</annotation></semantics></math>, and apply softmax and argmax operations to <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{S}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{S}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the predicted class per pixel.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04021v1/metaprogress_v4.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/198;" width="476" height="198" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example progression of reconstruction and segmentation performance as a MetaSeg INR fits the pixels of a test image.</span> We report PSNR (top) and Dice (bottom) scores. MetaSeg’s initialization leads to rapid convergence, with a Dice score of 0.85 emerge as early as <math id="S2.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{f}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{f}=2</annotation></semantics></math> iterations. It achieves optimal segmentation (Dice=<math id="S2.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math>, PSNR=<math id="S2.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="41.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>41.3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">41.3</annotation></semantics></math>) at <math id="S2.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{f}=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{f}=100</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, and eventually declines as it overfits to the image pixels at <math id="S2.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{f}=5000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{f}=5000</annotation></semantics></math> iterations.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experiments and Results</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated MetaSeg on the tasks of 2D and 3D MRI image segmentation. We used 414 T1 brain MRI scans from the OASIS-MRI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> dataset, randomly split into 214 training, 100 validation, and 100 test scans. OASIS-MRI provides 5 coarse (background, white matter, gray matter, cortex, and cerebrospinal fluid), 24 fine-grained segmentation labels for 2D slices, and 35 segmentation labels for full 3D MRI volumes. We used aligned coronal sections provided in OASIS-MRI for 2D segmentation and aligned 3D volumes for 3D segmentation. We normalized all images and volumes to have intensity in <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{bmatrix}0,1\end{bmatrix}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{bmatrix}0,1\end{bmatrix}</annotation></semantics></math>. We padded and resized images to resolution <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="192\times 192" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>192</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>192</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">192\times 192</annotation></semantics></math>, and cropped 3D volumes to <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="160\times 160\times 200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>160</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>160</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">160\times 160\times 200</annotation></semantics></math> followed by <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="2\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times</annotation></semantics></math> downsampling to yield <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80\times 80\times 100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>80</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80\times 80\times 100</annotation></semantics></math> resolution scans.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation.</span> For all experiments, we used a SIREN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> INR with <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=[6,5,5]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=[6,5,5]</annotation></semantics></math> layers, width <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=[128,512,256]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>128,512,256</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=[128,512,256]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{0}=30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{0}=30</annotation></semantics></math>. We set the segmentation head <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\phi}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\phi}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> as one fully connected layer with Leaky-ReLU activation, followed by a linear layer with <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> output values. We used the Adam <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> optimizer with a learning rate of <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> for inner and outer meta-learning optimizations. When tuning the segmentation head for 5 classes, we reduced the learning rate to <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5e-5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5e-5</annotation></semantics></math>. We set <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{f}=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{f}=100</annotation></semantics></math>, and ran the outer loop for <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{o}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{o}=10</annotation></semantics></math> epochs over the training data while validating the performance of optimized parameters <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{t},\phi^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{t},\phi^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> every <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> steps. For 2D segmentation with 5 classes, we set the focal loss hyperparameter <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, and for 24 output classes we set <math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=2.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=2.0</annotation></semantics></math>. For 3D MRI volumes which exhibit high background class imbalance, we set <math id="S3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=3.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=3.0</annotation></semantics></math> and further scaled the reconstruction loss by <math id="S3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.1</annotation></semantics></math> for background pixels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines:</span> We adopt a widely used U-Net <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> baseline for 2D MRIs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> and SegResNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> from the Monai <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> package for 3D MRIs. We trained all models using a learning rate of <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1e-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1e-3</annotation></semantics></math> until the validation loss saturated. We also compare against NISF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, the most closely related INR method in the literature.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span><span id="S3.T1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quantitative performance of MetaSeg and baseline models on 2D and 3D MRI segmentation tasks.</span> MetaSeg achieves comparable Dice scores to popular U-Net baselines with <math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>90</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90\%</annotation></semantics></math> fewer parameters.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Task</td>
<td id="S3.T1.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Num. classes</td>
<td id="S3.T1.4.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Model</td>
<td id="S3.T1.4.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Dice Score <math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.4.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">Num. parameters <math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
